# ADR-003 — Content Identity, Source and Provenance

**Status:** Proposed — freeze pending sign-off (Gate 0 not complete). Approval happens outside this ADR, in the PR-00 review of the seven-ADR set (plan §1, §19 preamble); until that sign-off, every decision here stays a proposal and nothing in the repository reflects it.

**Plan:** `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-17-feedmine-runtime-v2-revised.md` §4 row 1, §5, §7, §19 rows 1–14, 28, 31, 40, §20.2.
**Semantic authority:** Blueprint v0.4 §20–§44, §59–§63, §112, §122 (SHA-256 `b230342258566a2366dd07cd64eea5672504e2f7dff3b3b7f3fa7a55a07344e7`).
**Subordinate technical reference:** Technical Architecture v0.1 §8, §9, §10, §11, §107 (SHA-256 `f8484da4da0ff3778728cfc7ba0edba785ceb7ca257ff7ee9c8ab84d86cb29c3`).

## Context

The decision this ADR closes is: what an identity *is* in Runtime V2 — which identity is editorial, which is operational, which is durable across databases and devices, and which is merely an observation. The Blueprint states the target semantics: `Source` is a FeedMine editorial unit and not an endpoint (§20), `SourceID` is FeedMine-owned and no longer derived from a canonical URL (§22), `SourceID ≠ ProviderID` (§21), `SourceBinding`/`AcquisitionTarget` are operational and not 1:1 (§23–§26), external identities are opaque and namespaced at the connector boundary (§27), record identity is distinct from revision identity and no universal revision counter exists (§28–§30), provenance is separate from membership (§36–§37), and canonicalization must stay additive and reversible (§39–§44, §112 principle). Technical Architecture §8 fixes the split this ADR must encode: hot-path IDs small and integer (`SourceID`/`ProviderID` `UInt64`, row IDs `Int64`, §8), external identity opaque with only an auxiliary fingerprint (§9), and `OriginRevision` carries `authoredAt?`/`modifiedAt?`/`observedAt` (§10).

Five facts in this checkout force the decision, and two of them are in direct tension with the target semantics:

- The app already has a different `SourceID`: `feedmine/FeedEngine/Identities.swift:32-37` declares `struct SourceID { let rawValue: UInt32 }` and `:72-74` reserves `SourceID(rawValue: 0)` as `none`. The catalog compiles that ID as the first 32 bits of a SHA-256 of the canonical URL (`feedmine/FeedEngine/CatalogIdentity.swift:20-21`, digest truncation at `:55-62`), i.e. exactly the `SourceID(hash(url))` form the Blueprint rejects in §22 and the Technical Architecture lists as an architecture defect (§108). The catalog read path additionally narrows an `Int64` row id through `UInt32(rawID)` (`feedmine/FeedEngine/SQLiteCatalogStore.swift:812-813`).
- The catalog is built into a temporary file and swapped in, with `catalog_source.key TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE` and a textual key alongside its integer PK (`feedmine/FeedEngine/SQLiteCatalogStore.swift:656-658`); collisions are already a hard error (`:211-217`). A runtime identity that mirrors the catalog row id reallocates on every rebuild.
- `FeedSource.id` and `SourceReference.id` are both `OPMLParser.normalizeURL(url)` (`feedmine/Models/FeedSource.swift:14`, `:125`) — a URL-derived identity that the existing identity contract tests treat as authoritative for fetch identity, including stripping signed parameters (`feedmineTests/CatalogIdentityContractTests.swift:42-47`).
- The legacy item identity is a single opaque string: `FeedItem.generateID` returns SHA-256 of `sourceURL|guid_or_link`, choosing `guid`, then `link`, then `title|publishedAt` (`feedmine/Models/FeedItem.swift:393-401`), the raw GUID survives only as a local parameter of the fetcher (`feedmine/Services/RSSFetcher.swift:943`, `:972-978`) and is not persisted — `FeedItem` (`feedmine/Models/FeedItem.swift:2-27`) and `FeedItemRecord` (`feedmine/Services/FeedStore.swift:8037-8065`) have no raw external key column, while `feed_item` is keyed by that TEXT id (`feedmine/Services/FeedStore.swift:7470-7471`). With it, the fetcher drops any item without a link (`feedmine/Services/RSSFetcher.swift:960`) or without a non-empty sanitized title (`:967`), silently losing content the Blueprint admits (§33, §34).
- Durable user state already references that TEXT id: `bookmark_item.item_id` is TEXT with PK `(list_id, item_id)` (`feedmine/Services/UserStateStore.swift:214-217`) and hydration joins `feed_item.id` (`feedmine/Services/BookmarkStore.swift:128-134`), while `feed_item` rows are deleted by retention except bookmarks (`feedmine/Services/FeedStore.swift:7256-7261`). `imported_source.source_identity` is already a unique durable identity key (`feedmine/Services/UserStateStore.swift:377-380`).

## Decision

**D1 — Source is the editorial unit, and its ID is FeedMine-owned.** A Source is the unit FeedMine catalogs, enables, selects and presents (Blueprint §20). `FeedDomain.SourceID` is an opaque `UInt64` wrapper allocated and persisted by the runtime; it is never derived from a URL, endpoint, account, protocol object or external key (Blueprint §22). `0` is not a valid `SourceID`.

**D2 — The catalog ID is a different type, aliased, never converted.** The app-level `FeedEngine.SourceID` (`UInt32`, `Identities.swift:32-37`) is and remains the *catalog* identity. Bridges refer to it as `CatalogSourceID`; there is no `init` on `FeedDomain.SourceID` taking a `CatalogSourceID`, no `UInt32`/`UInt64` narrowing on the path, and no computation of a runtime ID from a URL digest. The only translation is a persisted lookup in `legacy_source_map` (D18), which fails when no mapping exists.

**D3 — Local row IDs are positive `Int64` with a checked encoding.** Every runtime table that stores a local identity uses `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY` in the positive `Int64` range, allocated by SQLite/GRDB, with `CHECK (id > 0)` so `0` stays reserved and unreachable. Legacy values enter through a checked conversion that throws on `0`, negatives and out-of-range values; truncating casts of the kind found at `SQLiteCatalogStore.swift:812-813` are forbidden in `FeedDomain`, `FeedStorage` and the bridges (Technical Architecture §8).

**D4 — Durable editorial key is separate from the local row ID.** Durable identity is `EditorialSourceKey(catalogIdentity, canonicalizationVersion)` — a value key, not a number — persisted with the `source` row and never reused as a foreign key. The runtime persists catalog identity + canonicalization version → runtime source (D19). A catalog rebuild re-applies those mappings and never reallocates runtime IDs by row order or by re-derivation from the URL.

**D5 — Source, Provider, Binding, Target are four distinct things.** `Source` is editorial (D1). `Provider` is attribution/authorship used for diversity and display (Blueprint §21, §38) — one Source contains many Providers and vice versa. `SourceBinding` is the declarative relation between a Source and an external system, carrying connector namespace, connector-owned configuration, enabled/revoked state and a monotonic `generation` (Blueprint §23, §26). `AcquisitionTarget` is operational work and may be shared by many bindings (Blueprint §24, §25); it is owned by ADR-005.

**D6 — Endpoint is an attribute of a binding, not of a Source.** Changing a binding's endpoint or configuration keeps the same `SourceID` and increments `binding.generation`; it does not create a Source. Continuity is asserted by an explicit mapping/alias (D19), never inferred from URL similarity.

**D7 — Target identity never becomes editorial identity.** The runtime stores targets and observations, and may reference a target from provenance, but no query derives selection or attribution identity from a target id (D15).

**D8 — External keys are opaque, scoped, and compared in full.** `ExternalObjectKey` and `ExternalVersionKey` are opaque `Data` (Technical Architecture §9). Their key space is `(connectorNamespace, scopeKey, keyKind)`, where `scopeKey` identifies the origin/account/feed scope the connector declares. Uniqueness is over namespace + scope + keyKind + the **full** key bytes. The 128-bit digest is an auxiliary index only; equality decisions compare the complete key. The core never interprets key contents (Blueprint §27, §30).

**D9 — `ExternalVersionKey` is not globally ordered.** There is no `universalRevisionNumber` (Blueprint §30). Version keys are opaque and unordered; ordering inside Runtime V2 comes from the connector's explicit precedence instruction applied by Admission (ADR-006, Blueprint §63), which may also be `historicalOnly` or `duplicate`.

**D10 — An RSS/Atom GUID is not a URL.** A GUID is stored, compared and replayed as an opaque byte string, even when it is spelled like a URI. Runtime V2 does not rewrite its scheme, strip or sort its query parameters, merge hosts, percent-decode/normalize it, or resolve it relative to the feed. `OPMLParser.normalizeURL` remains legitimate for *source/fetch* URL identity (`FeedSource.swift:14`) and the contract tests that pin it remain valid; it is never applied to an external object key.

**D11 — Version collisions are auditable conflicts, never overwrites.** If an admitted observation carries an `externalVersionKey` already attached to another revision of the same record with a divergent payload digest, the stored revision is preserved unchanged, the incoming revision is either admitted under its own version key or rejected, and an `identity_conflict` row of kind `version_payload_divergence` records both digests. A failed/aborted insert is the detection point, not an error to swallow.

**D12 — Aliases add evidence; ambiguous aliases never merge.** Additional keys that resolve to the same record are stored as additional `external_identity` rows (aliases) pointing at that `origin_record`. If a key is already attached to a different record in the same scope, or two records claim one alias, neither record is deleted, merged or rewritten: the conflicting mapping is refused and recorded as `ambiguous_alias`. Alias rows carry their provenance; a merge would require a separate, explicit, reversible decision (D13).

**D13 — Equivalence is a relation, not a merge.** Cross-protocol syndication is expressed with `content_entity_member` (strong equivalence inside an explicit trust domain) and `content_cluster_member` (soft similarity with `confidence`, `method`, `version`). `origin_record` and its revisions are never destroyed to form an entity or a cluster; deleting the relation rows restores the split (Blueprint §39, §40).

**D14 — Only promoted relations are canonical.** `content_relation` accepts exactly `replyTo`, `repostOf`, `quoteOf`, `references`, and only when the relation changes how FeedMine selects or presents content (Blueprint §41, §42). Any other external relation is retained as connector evidence and is invisible to Selection, Publication and Presentation (Blueprint §92, §93).

**D15 — Membership and target observation are different relations.** `source_membership` records an editorial association and preserves how it was known (`membership_kind`, evidence target, binding generation). `target_observation` records that a target produced an observation of an external identity. Neither implies the other: observing an object through a target creates no membership, and a membership creates no observation (Blueprint §36, §37).

**D16 — Missing external identity falls back to a versioned, low-confidence key.** When a connector has no GUID/Atom id and no usable primary link, identity uses the connector's explicit fallback scheme, versioned (`fallbackSchemeVersion`) and stored with `identity_confidence = 'low'`. Two different pieces of content that share title and date produce two records; a collision never deletes, overwrites or discards content. Missing headline and missing primary link are admissible and never cause rejection (Blueprint §33, §34).

**D17 — Timestamp epistemology is preserved.** `authoredAt` and `modifiedAt` are nullable and are written only when the connector declares them; `observedAt` is always the local FeedMine observation time. No code path substitutes `Date()` or `observedAt` for an absent `authoredAt` (Blueprint §35). A sort-date fallback (`authoredAt ?? observedAt`) is an explicit, recorded policy input, not a rewritten field.

**D18 — Legacy bridges are the only legacy identity path.** `legacy_source_map` maps catalog source identity (key + id + canonicalization version) to a runtime source; `legacy_item_map` maps the legacy TEXT item id to an `origin_record`/`origin_revision` with an explicit confidence. While the coexistence window of ADR-004 holds, runtime IDs are never used as the bookmark key: bookmarks keep the legacy durable key and snapshots (plan §5.2, `BookmarkStore.swift:128-134`).

**D19 — No array index and no batch ordinal is an identity.** Nothing durable keys on array position, row order, batch order or viewport index. Ordinals exist only inside an acquisition batch or a published edition, are regenerated per batch/edition, and always accompany a real identity (`PublicationCardID`, `OriginRecordID`, durable action key — ADR-001/004/007).

## Rejected alternatives

- **Keep deriving `SourceID` from the canonical URL digest (`CatalogIdentity.swift:20-21,55-62`).** Rejected: a 32-bit URL digest cannot satisfy Blueprint §22 (stable across redirects, endpoint changes and binding changes), recreates the legacy `SourceID(hash(url))` defect listed in Technical Architecture §108, and makes two independently rebuilt catalogs alias unrelated sources on a 32-bit collision.
- **Reuse the catalog integer row id as the runtime `SourceID`.** Rejected: the catalog is rebuilt into a temporary file and swapped (`SQLiteCatalogStore.swift:656-658`), so row-id reuse makes identity positional; it also recreates the `UInt32(Int64)` narrowing seen at `SQLiteCatalogStore.swift:812-813`.
- **Convert `UInt32` catalog ID into `UInt64` runtime ID by widening.** Rejected: it silently declares that the two identities are the same thing. The catalog ID is a projection key; the runtime must resolve through `legacy_source_map` and fail loudly when unmapped.
- **Normalize external keys with the source-URL normalizer (strip auth params, unify scheme/www/trailing slash).** Rejected: it would rewrite opaque GUIDs into different identities (D10), destroying the ability to replay a collision audit, and it would merge distinct objects that share a host.
- **Merge syndicated duplicates into one record during canonicalization.** Rejected: irreversible, and it deletes the provenance that Selection needs for provider diversity and that diagnostics need for replay (Blueprint §39, §40).
- **Make `externalVersionKey` an ordered revision counter so "newest wins" is cheap.** Rejected: no external system guarantees global or per-record ordering; Blueprint §30 and §63 make precedence a connector instruction applied per observation.
- **Treat a target's observation as membership to avoid a second table.** Rejected: it is exactly the conflation of operational provenance with editorial membership that Blueprint §36–§37 exists to prevent, and it would let a shared target silently enroll content in an unrelated Source.

## Schema and types

SQLite fragments are the proposed minimum for `runtime-v2.sqlite` (plan §6 group "Identidade" and "Canônico"); GRDB migrations are the schema authority, `DatabasePool` + WAL + foreign keys on every connection. Ordinal columns are intentionally absent from these tables (D19).

```sql
-- D1, D3, D4: Source is editorial, ID is runtime-owned, durable key is separate.
CREATE TABLE source (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY CHECK (id > 0),          -- 0 reserved for "none"; never a row
    editorial_key TEXT NOT NULL,                    -- durable catalog identity
    canonicalization_version INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (canonicalization_version > 0),
    display_title TEXT NOT NULL,
    kind TEXT,                                      -- deliberately not enumerated yet (Blueprint §20)
    created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    CHECK (length(editorial_key) > 0),
    UNIQUE (editorial_key, canonicalization_version)
);
-- D5, D6: Provider is attribution, never an editorial grouping substitute.
CREATE TABLE provider (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY CHECK (id > 0),
    connector_namespace TEXT NOT NULL,              -- 'feedmine.local' for catalog/personal rows
    provider_key TEXT NOT NULL,                     -- durable, namespace-scoped
    display_name TEXT NOT NULL,
    created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    UNIQUE (connector_namespace, provider_key)
);
-- D5, D6, D7: binding is the declarative relation Source ↔ external system.
-- acquisition_target.id is owned by ADR-005; only the reference is fixed here.
CREATE TABLE source_binding_runtime (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY CHECK (id > 0),
    source_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES source(id),
    connector_namespace TEXT NOT NULL,
    binding_key TEXT NOT NULL,                      -- connector-owned external principal
    generation INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 1 CHECK (generation >= 1),  -- D6, ADR-006
    enabled INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 1 CHECK (enabled IN (0, 1)),
    config_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',
    created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    updated_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    UNIQUE (connector_namespace, binding_key)
);
CREATE INDEX idx_source_binding_source ON source_binding_runtime (source_id, enabled);
-- D12: alias keys stay scoped and evidenced; a refused alias writes identity_conflict.
CREATE TABLE source_binding_alias (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY CHECK (id > 0),
    binding_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES source_binding_runtime(id),
    alias_key TEXT NOT NULL,
    first_observed_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    UNIQUE (binding_id, alias_key)
);
-- D8, D9, D10, D12: one row per opaque key; digest is auxiliary.
CREATE TABLE external_identity (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY CHECK (id > 0),
    connector_namespace TEXT NOT NULL,
    scope_key TEXT NOT NULL,                        -- origin / account / feed scope
    key_kind TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (key_kind IN ('object', 'version')),
    external_key BLOB NOT NULL,                     -- full key, opaque, compared as bytes
    key_digest BLOB NOT NULL,                       -- 128-bit auxiliary index only
    origin_record_id INTEGER REFERENCES origin_record(id),
    identity_confidence TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'high'
        CHECK (identity_confidence IN ('high', 'low')),
    fallback_scheme_version INTEGER,                -- non-NULL only when confidence = 'low'
    first_observed_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    last_observed_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    CHECK (length(external_key) > 0),
    CHECK (key_kind <> 'object' OR origin_record_id IS NOT NULL),
    CHECK (identity_confidence <> 'low' OR fallback_scheme_version IS NOT NULL),
    UNIQUE (connector_namespace, scope_key, key_kind, external_key)
);
CREATE INDEX idx_external_identity_digest
    ON external_identity (connector_namespace, scope_key, key_kind, key_digest);
-- D11, D12: divergence and ambiguity are recorded, never resolved by overwrite.
CREATE TABLE identity_conflict (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY CHECK (id > 0),
    connector_namespace TEXT NOT NULL,
    scope_key TEXT NOT NULL,
    conflict_kind TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (conflict_kind IN (
        'version_payload_divergence', 'ambiguous_alias', 'digest_collision',
        'key_kind_mismatch', 'legacy_map_conflict')),
    existing_origin_record_id INTEGER REFERENCES origin_record(id),
    existing_identity_id INTEGER REFERENCES external_identity(id),
    incoming_key_digest BLOB NOT NULL,
    detail_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',
    detected_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    resolved_at INTEGER,
    CHECK (resolved_at IS NULL OR resolved_at >= detected_at)
);
-- D8, D9, D16: logical external object.
CREATE TABLE origin_record (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY CHECK (id > 0),
    connector_namespace TEXT NOT NULL,
    scope_key TEXT NOT NULL,
    primary_identity_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES external_identity(id),
    availability TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'available' CHECK (availability IN (
        'available', 'updated', 'removed', 'revoked', 'unknown')),
    current_revision_id INTEGER REFERENCES origin_revision(id),
    first_observed_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    last_observed_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    CHECK (last_observed_at >= first_observed_at)
);
-- D9, D17: append-only representation. UPDATE is refused by trigger; DELETE only by
-- an explicit ADR-004 retention policy, and never while a publication references it.
CREATE TABLE origin_revision (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY CHECK (id > 0),
    origin_record_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES origin_record(id),
    external_version_key BLOB,                      -- opaque, unordered; NULL when versionless
    payload_digest BLOB NOT NULL,                   -- what collision policy compares (D11)
    headline TEXT,                                  -- nullable by design (D16, Blueprint §33)
    summary TEXT,
    body_text TEXT,
    authored_at INTEGER,                            -- nullable, never synthesized (D17)
    modified_at INTEGER,
    observed_at INTEGER NOT NULL,                   -- always local
    language TEXT,
    primary_link TEXT,                              -- nullable, never synthesized (D18)
    search_projection TEXT,
    created_at INTEGER NOT NULL
);
CREATE INDEX idx_origin_revision_record ON origin_revision (origin_record_id, created_at, id);
-- D11 needs a uniqueness/index rule over (origin_record_id, external_version_key,
-- payload_digest) that admits a divergent payload for detection instead of aborting
-- blindly. No index is declared here on purpose: see the OPEN item below.
CREATE TRIGGER origin_revision_append_only
BEFORE UPDATE ON origin_revision
BEGIN
    SELECT RAISE(ABORT, 'origin_revision is append-only');
END;
-- Bootstrap ordering for the origin_record ↔ external_identity cycle:
-- insert the external_identity row with origin_record_id NULL, insert origin_record,
-- then backfill external_identity.origin_record_id in the same transaction. A crash
-- between the two leaves an identity row with no record; the next observation of the
-- same key reuses it and still creates exactly one record.
```

The `idx_origin_revision_version` line above is deliberately marked unusable and must **not** be shipped: it is a placeholder for the open question below. The collision policy (D11) needs a partial index that permits a divergent payload to be *detected* while a duplicate payload is a no-op; the exact predicate is `OPEN` (see below).

```sql
-- D15: editorial membership and operational observation, mutually non-implying.
CREATE TABLE source_membership (
    origin_record_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES origin_record(id),
    source_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES source(id),
    membership_kind TEXT NOT NULL,                  -- how it was known; not enumerated yet
    binding_id INTEGER REFERENCES source_binding_runtime(id),
    binding_generation INTEGER,
    first_observed_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    last_observed_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (origin_record_id, source_id, membership_kind)
);

CREATE TABLE target_observation (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY CHECK (id > 0),
    external_identity_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES external_identity(id),
    binding_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES source_binding_runtime(id),
    binding_generation INTEGER NOT NULL,
    acquisition_target_id INTEGER NOT NULL,         -- FK target owned by ADR-005
    admission_batch_id INTEGER,                     -- row owned by ADR-006
    observed_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    CHECK (binding_generation >= 1)
);
CREATE INDEX idx_target_observation_identity
    ON target_observation (external_identity_id, observed_at);
-- D5: revision → provider, with role and evidence.
CREATE TABLE provider_attribution (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY CHECK (id > 0),
    origin_revision_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES origin_revision(id),
    provider_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES provider(id),
    attribution_role TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (attribution_role IN ('primary', 'publisher', 'contributor')),
    evidence_key TEXT,
    created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    UNIQUE (origin_revision_id, provider_id, attribution_role)
);
-- D13: reversible equivalence and similarity.
CREATE TABLE content_entity_member (
    entity_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    origin_record_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES origin_record(id),
    method TEXT NOT NULL,
    version INTEGER NOT NULL,
    created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (entity_id, origin_record_id)
);

CREATE TABLE content_cluster_member (
    cluster_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    origin_record_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES origin_record(id),
    confidence REAL NOT NULL CHECK (confidence >= 0.0 AND confidence <= 1.0),
    method TEXT NOT NULL,
    version INTEGER NOT NULL,
    created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (cluster_id, origin_record_id)
);
-- D14: only promoted relations enter the canonical domain. `object_external_identity_id`
-- may be unresolved: an unknown target stays evidence.
CREATE TABLE content_relation (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY CHECK (id > 0),
    subject_origin_record_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES origin_record(id),
    relation TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (relation IN ('replyTo', 'repostOf', 'quoteOf', 'references')),
    object_external_identity_id INTEGER REFERENCES external_identity(id),
    object_origin_record_id INTEGER REFERENCES origin_record(id),
    created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    CHECK (object_external_identity_id IS NOT NULL OR object_origin_record_id IS NOT NULL),
    UNIQUE (subject_origin_record_id, relation, object_external_identity_id)
);
-- D18: durable bridges between legacy and runtime identity. Additive; coexist with
-- legacy readers for the whole compatibility window (ADR-004).
CREATE TABLE legacy_source_map (
    catalog_source_key TEXT NOT NULL,               -- catalog_source.key (SQLiteCatalogStore.swift:656-658)
    catalog_source_id INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (catalog_source_id > 0),
    canonicalization_version INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (canonicalization_version > 0),
    runtime_source_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES source(id),
    legacy_url TEXT NOT NULL,
    mapped_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (catalog_source_key, canonicalization_version)
);
CREATE INDEX idx_legacy_source_map_id
    ON legacy_source_map (catalog_source_id, canonicalization_version);

CREATE TABLE legacy_item_map (
    legacy_item_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,                -- FeedItem.generateID output (FeedItem.swift:393-401)
    legacy_source_url TEXT NOT NULL,
    origin_record_id INTEGER REFERENCES origin_record(id),
    origin_revision_id INTEGER REFERENCES origin_revision(id),
    confidence TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (confidence IN ('high', 'low', 'unresolved')),
    mapped_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    CHECK (length(legacy_item_id) > 0)
);
```

